    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0232",
      "sequence": 244,
      "meaning": "Astonished Sītā looked in all four directions, anxious where the royal youths had gone. Wherever her fawn-like eyes fell, it was as though rows of white lotuses rained down. Her friends then showed her, behind a screen of vines, the lovely dark and fair youths. At sight of their forms her eyes grew eager and she rejoiced as though recognizing her own treasure. Her eyes did not tire of looking at Raghupati’s beauty; they even abandoned blinking. Through deep affection she became heedless of her body, gazing like a partridge at the autumn moon. Bringing Rāma into her heart along the pathway of her eyes, the wise maiden closed the shutters of her eyelids. When the companions knew Sītā was overcome with love, she could say nothing and grew shy within. At that moment the two brothers emerged from the vine-bower like twin stainless moons coming out when a veil of cloud parts.",
