    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0235",
      "sequence": 247,
      "meaning": "Knowing Śiva’s bow was hard to string, Sītā grieved as she left, keeping the dark form in her heart. When the Lord saw Jānakī departing—mine of happiness, affection, beauty and virtues—he made tender ink of supreme love and inscribed her upon the lovely wall of his mind. She returned to Bhavānī’s shrine, bowed at her feet, joined her hands and prayed: ‘Victory, victory to the daughter of the mountain king; victory to the partridge devoted to the moon of Maheśa’s face. Victory to the mother of elephant-faced Gaṇeśa and six-faced Skanda, mother of the world, whose body has lightning’s radiance. You have no beginning, middle or end; your immeasurable power is unknown even to the Veda. You create existence, worldly abundance and dissolution; you enchant the universe and roam in complete freedom. Among faithful wives you, Mother, bear the foremost mark; a thousand Sarasvatīs and Śeṣas cannot tell your boundless glory.’",
